  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved capacity of the government and social partners to plan and manage social protection</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Syria&#x2019;s social insurance system remains a key anchor for post-conflict recovery. Despite widespread institutional collapse, it continued to collect contributions and pay benefits, preserving one of the few enduring links of trust between citizens and the State. This makes it a strategic entry point for rebuilding broader social protection.
However, the system faces severe financial and structural pressures. Limited reserve accumulation&#x2014;due to past legal constraints&#x2014;combined with delayed government contributions eroded by hyperinflation, has driven current imbalances and liquidity shortages. At the same time, high inflation, population ageing, and longer benefit durations are increasing costs without a corresponding expansion of contributors. Without urgent action, sustainability is at risk.
