However, the system faces severe financial and structural pressures. Limited reserve accumulation&#x2014;due to past legal constraints&#x2014;combined with delayed government contributions eroded by hyperinflation, has driven current imbalances and liquidity shortages. At the same time, high inflation, population ageing, and longer benefit durations are increasing costs without a corresponding expansion of contributors. Without urgent action, sustainability is at risk.

To respond, the ILO is supporting the National Social Security Institution in conducting its first actuarial assessment in 15 years, planned for early 2026. This will inform urgent reforms to stabilise liquidity and restore financial sustainability, alongside legal updates, digital and MIS upgrades, improved data and financial reporting, and stronger governance.

A second, more comprehensive actuarial assessment is planned for late 2026 or early 2027, building on improved data and systems to sustain the reform trajectory.</narrative>
  </description>
